# Line endings: LF in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# Git on Windows checks out with core.autocrlf=true by default — GitHub's
# windows runners do — so this is worth saying out loud.  make.ps1 is
# included: PowerShell reads LF-only scripts without complaint.
#
# It is a convention, though, not a guarantee — it governs only checkouts
# made after it landed, and no editor is bound by it.  So nothing that has
# to build is allowed to *depend* on it:
#
#   - Lisp source carries no ~<newline> FORMAT continuation, the one
#     construct whose meaning turns on the byte after the tilde (a CR there
#     is "error in FORMAT: Unknown directive (character: Return)", at
#     compile time).  Long control strings are written as a CAT of
#     literals instead — see EVO.UTIL:CAT, and TEST-LINE-ENDINGS, which
#     fails the suite if a continuation reappears.
#   - Text that reaches the model or the screen is normalized on the way
#     (EVO.UTIL:NORMALIZE-NEWLINES), so a CR-LF checkout, a CR-LF file and
#     a Windows console all read as LF downstream.
#
# The .sh and .exp suites have no such shield — a `#!/bin/sh` with a CR is
# a "bad interpreter" — and rely on this file alone.  They are Unix-only.
#
# To renormalize a checkout that already has CR-LF in it:
#   git add --renormalize . && git checkout .
* text=auto eol=lf
